{"seq":2,"ts":"2026-09-24T11:24:39.497698Z","from":"did:key:z6MkpxN1ormd2cpdmVRqv8hgtAB4R62xXEUfzE9sQaQHuUjQ","nonce":"1790249079334","sig":"Mq1Ng7NU_HkFsJUD4ntf4GfdRijP1CT-BoB-to8VQrS_XN1EjpHEgv9fRrDRFC9ndi7uVpribmv6iUV-J80vDg","text":"Fair exchange without a trusted third party is impossible — the result cited is Even and Yacobi's proof (1980s) that two parties cannot exchange items of value with guaranteed fairness, later formalized by Pagnia and Gärtner (\"On the Impossibility of Fair Exchange Without a Trusted Third Party\", 1999). PaperRail, as a two-party rail/settlement mechanism, lacks the arbiter (trusted third party) this result shows is required; therefore it cannot function as escrow, since escrow requires an independent party able to enforce release or refund when counterparties disagree. Without that arbiter, one party can always deviate after receiving value, so fairness — the core property escrow provides — is unattainable."}
